1
00:00:01,008 --> 00:00:04,090
If you've ever worked in the finance, healthcare,

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:08,819
or even just a global company with strict compliance rules,

3
00:00:08,909 --> 00:00:11,810
you have probably heard someone say we need to

4
00:00:11,810 --> 00:00:14,970
ensure data never leaves this region, and in the cloud,

5
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:18,549
enforcing that manually, project by project,

6
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:21,500
service by service is a recipe for mistake.

7
00:00:21,649 --> 00:00:24,370
Let's see how we can do it in the best possible way.

8
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:27,673
I'm inside the Google Cloud console, and at the top,

9
00:00:27,773 --> 00:00:29,972
let me select my organization.

10
00:00:30,093 --> 00:00:31,013
This is important.

11
00:00:31,173 --> 00:00:34,562
This setting must be applied at the organization or

12
00:00:34,562 --> 00:00:36,932
folder level to apply consistently.

13
00:00:37,053 --> 00:00:42,402
Now let me go to the IAM, and here I go to the Organization policies.

14
00:00:42,693 --> 00:00:47,312
What you are looking at here is essentially a master list of

15
00:00:47,312 --> 00:00:50,013
guardrails for your entire cloud environment.

16
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:54,405
Each policy here is like a rule you define once and

17
00:00:54,405 --> 00:01:00,055
Google Cloud enforces everywhere, no matter which team or project is involved.

18
00:01:00,206 --> 00:01:03,995
Let's begin with one most common restricting region policy.

19
00:01:04,135 --> 00:01:09,416
Let's say you are a company that must keep all the data inside Asia

20
00:01:09,416 --> 00:01:13,966
South 1 or Asia Southeast 1 due to the compliance rules.

21
00:01:14,445 --> 00:01:15,805
Now how we can enforce that?

22
00:01:15,896 --> 00:01:25,970
So for that, let's just search gcp.resourceLocations. Now I can see that.

23
00:01:26,099 --> 00:01:27,190
Let me click on that.

24
00:01:27,290 --> 00:01:31,169
Right now, it is inactive, there is no enforcement.

25
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:36,220
Let me click on these three dots and say Edit policy. This

26
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:39,459
constraint controls where resources can be created.

27
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:44,959
I'm here saying override parent's policy, and I'm saying add a rule.

28
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,260
In the rule, I will say Custom.

29
00:01:49,639 --> 00:01:59,290
I'm saying Allow only if the region is asia‑south‑1 or

30
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:08,029
asia‑southeast1. So I'm just saying that I want this organization to

31
00:02:08,029 --> 00:02:12,419
allow creating the resources only in these two regions,

32
00:02:13,270 --> 00:02:14,809
say Done, and say Set policy.

33
00:02:15,029 --> 00:02:18,089
From now on, no virtual machine, no bucket,

34
00:02:18,509 --> 00:02:23,809
no SQL databases, no managed service can be created outside these regions.

35
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:28,830
If someone tries to spin a VM in let's say us‑central‑1,

36
00:02:28,990 --> 00:02:30,350
they will see an error,

37
00:02:30,589 --> 00:02:34,093
maybe saying like constraint GCP resource location

38
00:02:34,093 --> 00:02:36,533
prohibits the creation of this resource.

39
00:02:36,772 --> 00:02:39,203
It looks like this is a small change,

40
00:02:39,533 --> 00:02:44,492
but actually this is how organizations enforce the GDPR,

41
00:02:44,772 --> 00:02:49,673
HIPAA, and all those different types of compliances based on their industry.

42
00:02:49,843 --> 00:02:50,623
Let's go back.

43
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:55,132
Not only this, we can also add a couple of more different policies as well.

44
00:02:55,813 --> 00:03:00,546
For example, let's say I want to disable the external IPs.

45
00:03:00,906 --> 00:03:05,035
Sometimes developers love to create virtual machines with public IPs

46
00:03:05,035 --> 00:03:07,276
because it's a little bit convenient for them,

47
00:03:07,436 --> 00:03:09,035
but attackers love it.

48
00:03:09,076 --> 00:03:12,555
And because of that, many times organizations want to disable that.

49
00:03:12,705 --> 00:03:15,055
How we can do that? So here under the filter,

50
00:03:15,475 --> 00:03:20,115
you search for compute.vmExternalIpAddress.

51
00:03:20,386 --> 00:03:23,955
Now here you can see that this policy is also inactive.

52
00:03:24,055 --> 00:03:29,098
What we can do is we can click on these three dots, say Edit policy, and we

53
00:03:29,098 --> 00:03:32,718
say overwrite parent's policy saying that Add a rule,

54
00:03:32,938 --> 00:03:35,979
Deny all, and say Set policy.

55
00:03:36,179 --> 00:03:39,938
Now the effect of this will be if someone tries to create a

56
00:03:39,938 --> 00:03:43,429
VM and choose assign a new external IP,

57
00:03:43,498 --> 00:03:47,738
the deployment will fail until and unless a folder or

58
00:03:47,738 --> 00:03:50,039
a project overrides this policy.

59
00:03:50,208 --> 00:03:53,792
Now let's quickly confirm how these policies flow down

60
00:03:53,792 --> 00:03:55,712
to the folders and the projects.

61
00:03:55,921 --> 00:03:58,322
Let me here click and get into the project.

62
00:03:58,432 --> 00:04:00,522
Here is our project, payments‑prod.

63
00:04:00,761 --> 00:04:03,121
Now here under the payments‑prod project,

64
00:04:03,281 --> 00:04:06,102
if you see the organization's policy here also,

65
00:04:06,121 --> 00:04:09,212
you will find the same policy has been active.

66
00:04:09,322 --> 00:04:12,542
For example, if I search here constraints location,

67
00:04:12,682 --> 00:04:16,721
you can see that. This policy is active here because the

68
00:04:16,721 --> 00:04:19,361
source is Inherit parent's policy.

69
00:04:19,682 --> 00:04:25,304
Similarly, if I search for compute.vmExternalIpAccess,

70
00:04:25,424 --> 00:04:28,714
you can see that this policy is also active,

71
00:04:28,744 --> 00:04:32,904
and you can see that the source is again Inherit parent's policy.

72
00:04:33,024 --> 00:04:38,804
This cascading behavior is exactly why a solid hierarchy is so powerful.

73
00:04:38,904 --> 00:04:41,975
You don't enforce the compliance project by project,

74
00:04:42,304 --> 00:04:44,885
but you did it at the organizational level,

75
00:04:45,095 --> 00:04:47,184
and it will flow down automatically.
